+# FAA follow-up: two original report copies acquired, but no case-level correction or disposition recovered
+
+October 29, 2026. B129 / FAA PD01–PD04, especially the PD03 follow-up trail. This unit acquired the complete archived PDF bodies of two DOT OIG semiannual reports that earlier work had only accessed through indexed text. Their relevant tables and pages can now be checked visually. The reports do **not** supply the actual corrected Commission answer, delivery acknowledgment, signed action/no-action determination or case-specific recommendation tracker.
+
+## Sources and acquisition
+
+| Source | Cover period | Acquired PDF | SHA-256 |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| S06, [official URL](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/sar_2006.pdf), [archived body](https://web.archive.org/web/20150907012317id_/https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/sar_2006.pdf) | April 1–September 30, 2006 | 78 pages; 8,045,353 bytes | e6ce6dc3248817dbccfd84ab1d2faffff385633a092a4473f116b948d140e0c3 |
+| S07, [official URL](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/trans4_SAR1H_09_072307_508_compliant.pdf), [archived body](https://web.archive.org/web/20150907191257id_/https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/trans4_SAR1H_09_072307_508_compliant.pdf) | October 1, 2006–March 31, 2007 | 112 pages; 6,774,721 bytes | 55ec54c800706dc94dbc49f7c604de9bf867e91ec78042c536cf2ed0416b28ae |
+
+Both archived bodies were acquired October 29, 2026. Each computed SHA-1, encoded as base32, matches its public CDX capture digest. That is a useful check tying the downloaded entity to the indexed capture; it is not native authoring, signature or case-record authentication. The separate CDX record-length fields are not treated as downloaded-PDF lengths. Failed live downloads remain preserved as 403 error responses, separately from the successful archive bodies. Access failure is not evidence of suppression.
+
+[SOURCES.json](SOURCES.json) records exact access times, capture identifiers, byte/hash/page checks, URLs and reading scope. Originals, derivatives and error responses remain unchanged. Only research notes and code are included in this public addition; the raw reports, images and extracted text are excluded.
+
+## What the actual 2006 report establishes
+
+**S06 physical page 46 / printed page 39**, heading *Results of OIG Investigation of 9/11 Commission Staff Referral*, dated **August 31, 2006**, contains the agency's summary of its investigation. It reports both of the following:
+
+- OIG did not find evidence sufficient to conclude that FAA officials knowingly made false statements, purposely omitted accurate information, or intentionally failed to correct an inaccurate statement after becoming aware of it.
+- OIG discovered that **three FAA executives did not act to correct** an erroneous Commission QFR response after learning it was inaccurate. It recommended correction and consideration of appropriate administrative action for **two current executives**.
+
+Both statements remain side by side. A finding of inaction after awareness and a conclusion concerning proof of intent are distinct propositions; the summary does not itself establish motive. The three-executive and two-current-executive descriptions must not be silently collapsed into the same population or called a numerical contradiction. The body says that OIG **recommended** correction and consideration of action. It does not document a subsequently delivered correction, completed personnel decision or recipient acknowledgment.
+
+This is another agency publication about the same investigation, identified here through its heading, date and substantive account. It is not a second independent investigation or independent corroboration of the underlying events. It does not display the CC-2006-085 identifier in this passage; the source relationship is not inferred from an identifier match that does not exist. The previously held [CC-2006-085 report](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf) and [earlier follow-up review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/055ef54a857b46846d0038109bab7e108970f03a/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-postreport-disposition-2026-10-27/README.md) remain unchanged.
+
+## The unresolved-investigations table concerns other matters
+
+**S06 physical page 61 / printed page 54** is titled *Status of Unresolved Investigations Recommendations*, with an overall April–September 2006 reporting period. Its displayed subgroup is **For Semiannual Report Period of April 1, 2005–September 30, 2005**. All **seven visible recommendation rows** were read: two OST/OCIO rows, three RITA rows and two FAA rows.
+
+The two FAA recommendations concern pilots receiving medical disability benefits and revisions to an airman medical-certificate application. Their status wording says FAA work on open issues continues. Those rows are not the Commission QFR correction or the recommendation concerning executives. A generic FAA label or open-issues phrase cannot be assigned to this case. Adjacent physical pages 60 and 62 contain other statistical presentations and establish the table's page boundaries in this copy.
+
+This narrows the usefulness of a previously located source: it is an actual example of investigation-recommendation reporting, but its specific FAA rows do not provide the sought case disposition. Its limited entries also do not establish a complete inventory of every investigation recommendation, a selection rule, or that an unlisted recommendation was resolved, implemented, withheld or never made.
+
+## The 2007 report supplies aggregate context, not the missing decision
+
+**S07 physical page 3 / printed iii** places the unresolved-recommendations list under **Audits**, while the Investigations statistics begin at printed page 78. **Physical page 83 / printed 77**, under *Required Statements for Semiannual Report*, discusses significant revisions to management decisions and disagreements **regarding audits**. Its statements cannot be broadened into a certification that OIG had no disagreement about this investigation or that all investigative recommendations were implemented.
+
+The complete displayed Investigations statistical section, **physical pages 84–86 / printed 78–80**, contains aggregate administrative/judicial actions, pending-case profiles and resource/use statistics. Physical page 87 begins Awards and Recognition. This section supplies no case-level correction, action/no-action or recommendation-resolution entry for the Commission referral. Its aggregate counts cannot establish whether either of the two current executives was disciplined. The contents/section comparison does not establish why the 2006 investigation-status table is not reproduced there or what happened to any particular recommendation.
+
+**Incidental unresolved numerical difference:** on **S07 physical page 85 / printed 79**, the pending-investigations table, headed as of March 31, 2007, displays a total of **472**. Its ten operating-administration case counts also sum to **472**. On **physical page 86 / printed 80**, the following prose reports a pending caseload of **473**. Both values are preserved in [OBSERVATIONS.json](OBSERVATIONS.json); neither is substituted for the other. A scope, cutoff, definition or editorial difference is untested. This does not identify a missing case, connect the difference to CC-2006-085, establish a false certification or support an inference of concealment.
+
+## Actual tests, limitations and next records
+
+The substantive advance is acquisition and source-level inspection. The complete 2006 investigation-status table can now be ruled out as a **direct case-specific answer in its displayed rows**, while remaining useful context. The 2006 case summary preserves both adverse and limiting agency statements. The 2007 statistics are qualified against case-level and audit/investigation inference errors. No original administrative outcome has been recovered.
+
+Fourteen page images were visually read: S06 **1, 4, 46, 60–62**; S07 **1, 3, 79, 83–87**. Seventeen pages were rendered; S07 physical **80–82** were rendered but not visually read. The other pages were not reviewed comprehensively. All 190 pages had text extracted for limited navigation searches; a keyword miss is not proof of substantive absence. The S07 text extraction substitutes malformed characters for some visible digits, including in its heading and total; the visible 472 reading and the untouched text derivative are retained as different representations. No source text was repaired. No OCR, hidden-text reconstruction, independent second reader, native-email authentication or personnel-record examination was performed.
+
+[verify_records.py](verify_records.py) checks original bytes, hashes and page counts when supplied the preserved PDFs, and reproduces the selected ten-row sum. These checks do not authenticate historical decisions, identify people behind redactions or validate the agency's intent finding. [REVIEW-AND-CHECKS.json](REVIEW-AND-CHECKS.json) separates source acquisition, actual reading and inference limits; [TEXT-CHANGES.diff](TEXT-CHANGES.diff) preserves the additive research-note changes.
+
+**Still required:** PD01 actual corrected QFR and delivery/recipient receipt; PD02 signed consideration/action/no-action, reasons and notification; PD03 native investigation follow-up/resolution/implementation record and any report-to-tracker crosswalk; PD04 operative policy/version and case routing. For the 472/473 difference, the source table and prose preparation/definition records or a source correction could discriminate among explanations; they have not been obtained. The highest-value next case records remain the native FAA/OIG action notification, supporting decision and corrected-answer transmittal, including records of a decision declining action. No request was sent.
+
+All five original work-plan tracks and eighteen input/test requirements remain open under their original criteria; held telephone sources and completed recorder/materials/steel tests are not relabeled unperformed. No findings of conspiracy, intentional concealment, completed correction or completed discipline follow from this unit. No source or old finding was overwritten, and no private push, outreach, fee, new filing/request, mailbox access, merge, deletion or history rewrite occurred.
